WEBVTT

1
00:00:24.044 --> 00:00:29.889
Hello and welcome to the NPTEL MOOCs course on Foundation for Virtual and Augmented Reality Systems.

2
00:00:31.971 --> 00:00:36.475
We will be continuing our discussion on resource optimization.

3
00:00:37.176 --> 00:00:52.330
In particular, we are focusing on VR resource optimization, that is, resource optimization concerns that are typically encountered when we are dealing with immersive experiences. So earlier,

4
00:00:53.288 --> 00:00:58.514
in this lecture, in the other parts of this lecture, we talked about the basic idea of computer graphics.

5
00:01:00.096 --> 00:01:06.263
We know that rendering is the most challenging part of an immersive experience generation from the point of view of the system.

6
00:01:07.705 --> 00:01:14.072
So, we need to have a basic understanding of the rendering process, which is the field of computer graphics that deals with this.

7
00:01:15.094 --> 00:01:21.321
So, we are talking about computer graphics. Earlier we talked about the basic

8
00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:34.073
idea of the computer graphics field, what it is. We learned about a few terminologies, and we also introduced the 3D graphics pipeline and the stages in the pipeline.

9
00:01:34.834 --> 00:01:46.661
Among those stages, there are five stages as we have seen. Among those, we have covered so far four stages: the first stage, object representation; the second one, modeling transformation;

9.1
00:01:46.661 --> 00:01:50.751
the third one, lighting; and the fourth one, viewing transformation.

10
00:01:53.735 --> 00:02:03.867
In this lecture, what we are going to do is basically talk about the fifth and the last stage of the graphics pipeline, and also we will be talking about how to implement

10.1
00:02:03.867 --> 00:02:15.121
the pipeline implementation aspect from both the software point of view, where implementation can be considered in the form of libraries, and

10.1.1
00:02:15.121 --> 00:02:22.999
from the hardware point of view, that is, how the GPUs or graphics processing units help in faster rendering.

11
00:02:25.223 --> 00:02:32.879
So, just to put the matter into perspective, this is the 3D graphics pipeline.

12
00:02:33.861 --> 00:02:39.759
This is the first stage, object representation, which takes place in the local coordinate system,

12.1
00:02:39.759 --> 00:02:46.607
Then modeling or geometric transformation transforms from the local coordinate to the world coordinate system, then lighting,

13
00:02:47.752 --> 00:02:53.630
takes place in the world coordinate system. Then the view pipeline, comprising a viewing transformation that is

13.1
00:02:53.630 --> 00:03:03.533
a transformation from world to 3D view coordinate system, then a clipping operation which is a

14
00:03:04.238 --> 00:03:06.501
process which happens in the 3D view coordinate system.

15
00:03:06.902 --> 00:03:11.088
Then hidden surface removal again happens in the 3D view coordinate system.

16
00:03:11.929 --> 00:03:18.459
Then projection transformation, that is transformation from 3D view coordinate to 2D view coordinate system.

17
00:03:21.704 --> 00:03:29.976
Then window to viewport transformation, that is transforming from 2D view coordinate system to a device coordinate system.

18
00:03:30.563 --> 00:03:41.259
So, today in this part of the lecture, we are going to talk about the fifth stage, that is scan conversion, where we transform the device coordinate

18.1
00:03:41.259 --> 00:03:46.600
description to a screen coordinate description that is on the pixel grid.

19
00:03:50.605 --> 00:03:58.393
So, this stage, stage 5, is called the rendering stage, or it may also be called the scan conversion stage.

20
00:04:00.348 --> 00:04:04.692
So, all the terms are synonymous.

21
00:04:04.712 --> 00:04:09.257
Now, what is the basic problem that is dealt with in this stage?

22
00:04:10.298 --> 00:04:26.375
Now, through the four stages, what we have done so far is to describe and transform a 3D scene to a 2D viewport, which is defined on a device coordinate system.

23
00:04:27.376 --> 00:04:29.798
Now, when we are talking of a device coordinate system,

24
00:04:30.166 --> 00:04:36.395
essentially, the system is a continuous coordinate system.

25
00:04:36.455 --> 00:04:40.581
That means the coordinate values can be any real number.

26
00:04:42.825 --> 00:04:58.047
However, when we are talking about a device or display device, we know that it has a pixel grid which is used to render the image. Now,

27
00:04:59.208 --> 00:05:14.748
The problem here is to map from a continuous coordinate system, that is the device coordinate system, to a discrete coordinate system where the coordinates can only be integer values.

28
00:05:14.768 --> 00:05:21.857
Because on a pixel grid, the points must have integer coordinates, whereas in the

29
00:05:22.175 --> 00:05:26.480
viewport description, points can have any real number as coordinates.

30
00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:32.286
So, the objective is to transform from this continuous space to the discrete pixel grid space.

31
00:05:33.467 --> 00:05:37.652
The idea can be understood from this illustration here.

32
00:05:37.892 --> 00:05:45.200
As you can see, suppose each of these intersections represents a pixel.

33
00:05:47.062 --> 00:05:47.963
So, you have a line.

34
00:05:52.482 --> 00:05:55.747
And we want to represent it on this pixel grid.

35
00:05:57.470 --> 00:06:06.964
Now, as you can see, the line can pass through either a pixel or the interval between pixels.

36
00:06:08.326 --> 00:06:11.251
So, any real number is allowed as a coordinate.

37
00:06:11.271 --> 00:06:13.674
So, that is shown in the dotted line.

38
00:06:14.175 --> 00:06:19.283
However, when we are trying to render it on the display device,

39
00:06:20.191 --> 00:06:25.737
we have to choose the right pixels to color so that it represents the line.

40
00:06:26.798 --> 00:06:41.155
That means we can choose, suppose, this pixel, then this one, then this one, this one, and this one instead of the points that lie in between pixels.

41
00:06:43.117 --> 00:06:47.582
So, essentially, we are mapping from any real value to

42
00:06:48.136 --> 00:07:01.315
integer values representing the nearest pixel, and this process of mapping is the fifth stage, that is rendering or scan conversion, or even it is called rasterization sometimes.

43
00:07:04.439 --> 00:07:14.093
So, we need to map from the viewport, that is the continuous space, to the pixel grid, that is the discrete coordinate space.

44
00:07:14.495 --> 00:07:24.066
And these mapping algorithms or techniques are collectively known as the rendering stage, often called the scan conversion stage or rasterization stage.

45
00:07:27.950 --> 00:07:29.412
Now, how is it done?

46
00:07:30.192 --> 00:07:33.136
So, let us start with the simple case of point rendering.

47
00:07:34.237 --> 00:07:35.318
Here, it is very simple.

48
00:07:35.919 --> 00:07:44.248
If we have a real number as a coordinate to represent the point, then we can

49
00:07:44.971 --> 00:07:48.877
round off the real coordinates to the nearest integer values.

50
00:07:49.458 --> 00:08:04.141
For example, 2.3 and 2.6 can be rounded up to 2 and 3, which will be the mapping from the device space to the discrete space.

51
00:08:07.967 --> 00:08:11.653
However, that is good for points, but not good for

52
00:08:13.591 --> 00:08:18.457
other shapes, such as lines, circles, and any other geometric shapes.

53
00:08:22.382 --> 00:08:32.214
Now, line rendering is one of the most common activities that take place in image rendering under the purview of computer graphics.

54
00:08:32.354 --> 00:08:37.539
So, line rendering is the basis of any image rendering.

55
00:08:40.169 --> 00:08:52.609
Now if we want to render a line using a very naive intuitive method, then what happens is that we have to go for lots of calculations which are floating point operations.

56
00:08:53.410 --> 00:08:58.058
Now as we all know, floating point operations slow down a computer.

57
00:08:59.620 --> 00:09:06.852
So, the objective is to have efficient algorithms that can render a line by avoiding floating point operations.

58
00:09:07.237 --> 00:09:12.966
One such algorithm is Bresenham's line drawing algorithm, a quite popular and well-known algorithm.

59
00:09:16.051 --> 00:09:27.028
So, using this algorithm, what we want to do is find out the pixels that approximately represent the line as shown in this figure.

60
00:09:28.630 --> 00:09:31.354
So, we have the dotted line as the actual line.

61
00:09:31.394 --> 00:09:34.459
We need to identify pixels that will represent the line.

62
00:09:35.131 --> 00:09:39.998
And how do we find that without taking recourse to floating point operations?

63
00:09:40.018 --> 00:09:46.307
So, one such popular algorithm is Bresenham's line drawing algorithm.

64
00:09:46.327 --> 00:09:53.417
I am just referring to this algorithm to give you some idea of what kind of computations take place in this fifth stage.

65
00:09:56.481 --> 00:10:02.269
So, let us try to go through the algorithm for slopes which lie between 0 and 1.

66
00:10:06.181 --> 00:10:07.522
or between minus 1 and 0.

67
00:10:07.582 --> 00:10:09.944
So, effectively between minus 1 and plus 1.

68
00:10:12.607 --> 00:10:19.753
So, minor modification may be needed for any other slope.

69
00:10:19.773 --> 00:10:35.547
Now, here the input is the two line endpoints, this one and this one, and the output is the set of pixels p to render the line segment.

70
00:10:35.982 --> 00:10:39.005
That is the set of pixels that approximate the line.

71
00:10:40.947 --> 00:10:51.840
Now, at the beginning of the algorithm, we need to compute a few parameters, namely delta x, that is the difference between the two end point x coordinates,

71.1
00:10:51.840 --> 00:11:05.213
delta y, the difference between the two end point y coordinates, and p, a decision parameter which is twice delta y minus delta x. Then we start by initializing something, that is,

72
00:11:06.678 --> 00:11:22.501
x equal to the first endpoint, y equal to the first endpoint, and add this to the set of pixels p. Then, if the decision parameter value p is less than 0, that is this value that we have seen,

72.1
00:11:22.501 --> 00:11:36.128
then we increment x by 1 and reset the decision parameter as the older value plus twice delta y. Otherwise, we

73
00:11:36.496 --> 00:11:56.702
increment x by 1 and y by 1 and reset the value as p plus twice delta y minus twice delta x. And this goes on in a loop until we reach the other endpoint.

74
00:11:56.722 --> 00:12:02.790
So, at every loop instance, we add the computed coordinate value to the set of pixels.

75
00:12:04.272 --> 00:12:13.202
Now, here you can see that the entire operation is done based on integer arithmetic, addition, multiplication, and so on.

76
00:12:17.306 --> 00:12:28.759
So, this actually speeds up the rendering of lines, and this is one instance where we can have an efficient algorithm for faster line rendering.

77
00:12:28.799 --> 00:12:33.604
There are similar algorithms possible for other shapes as well.

78
00:12:34.681 --> 00:12:44.653
So, this algorithm is efficient because it avoids floating point operations such as rounding off and division, and also sometimes floating point multiplication.

79
00:12:44.673 --> 00:12:58.810
And avoiding these operations is very important to speed up the rendering because, at any given instant, we need to render a large number of lines or line segments simultaneously.

80
00:12:59.190 --> 00:13:03.996
So, speeding up certainly makes the system more efficient.

81
00:13:08.178 --> 00:13:14.865
Now, this algorithm we need to invoke for every line segment, and there may be thousands at any instant.

82
00:13:16.827 --> 00:13:30.018
For interactive graphics, where we can change the display based on user input such as in virtual reality, the computational need increases manifold because

82.1
00:13:30.018 --> 00:13:36.227
With every input, we need to change the scene, which means again redrawing hundreds of thousands of lines.

83
00:13:39.734 --> 00:13:44.301
So, such algorithms exist for other geometric shapes.

84
00:13:44.441 --> 00:13:56.861
Of course, those are a little bit more complex as compared to the line rendering shapes, such as circle, ellipse, fill area, or other polygons.

85
00:13:56.881 --> 00:13:57.622
We will skip those.

86
00:13:58.083 --> 00:14:06.636
We will not go into the details of those algorithms, and you may refer to the reference mentioned at the end.

87
00:14:07.510 --> 00:14:14.277
For more details, the basic idea is that we need some algorithm to efficiently render it on the screen.

88
00:14:14.998 --> 00:14:25.469
That means efficiently mapping the original shape to the set of pixels that will represent it on the screen.

89
00:14:25.489 --> 00:14:28.372
And our objective is to do it as fast as possible.

90
00:14:28.452 --> 00:14:33.197
That means reducing the floating point operations as much as possible.

91
00:14:33.898 --> 00:14:35.880
So for a line, we can completely eliminate them.

92
00:14:36.434 --> 00:14:44.503
But for other shapes, we may not be able to completely eliminate them, but we can minimize the number of operations.

93
00:14:44.523 --> 00:14:50.010
Now, there is one more concept we need to keep in mind, which is the aliasing effect.

94
00:14:50.030 --> 00:15:02.545
Remember that we are trying to get a set of pixels, such as in this line, which approximate the original line shown here in this figure.

95
00:15:02.985 --> 00:15:06.289
The original line is shown as a dotted line.

96
00:15:06.539 --> 00:15:14.269
Whereas the approximated line is shown as the thick continuous line.

97
00:15:17.914 --> 00:15:30.002
Now, as you can see, the approximate line does not exactly look like the original line because we are not exactly rendering the points on the line, but

97.1
00:15:30.002 --> 00:15:37.113
rendering pixels that are closer to those points on the line or closest to those points on the line.

98
00:15:37.113 --> 00:15:40.852
So, the shape may get distorted.

99
00:15:42.654 --> 00:15:51.007
So, some distortion may occur in the original shape after scan conversion; this is called aliasing.

100
00:15:51.027 --> 00:16:01.302
Now, what we need are additional operations to remove or reduce such distortion so that the end effect is better.

101
00:16:02.075 --> 00:16:07.041
Now, these operations collectively are known as anti-aliasing operations.

102
00:16:07.401 --> 00:16:17.594
So, there again, some sort of complex algorithms are there to take care of, or rather minimize, the effect of aliasing.

103
00:16:17.614 --> 00:16:27.986
If we do not do anything, then this aliasing may lead to distortion which will be visible with change in resolution, and that will certainly reduce the quality of experience.

104
00:16:28.006 --> 00:16:31.170
So, anti-aliasing is essential, and it

105
00:16:32.652 --> 00:16:42.403
introduces even more computations to the overall computation of pixel intensities along with the other stages.

106
00:16:48.090 --> 00:16:54.017
So, the previous discussion was only meant to give some idea of the amount of computations.

107
00:16:54.117 --> 00:17:00.364
Of course, here we skipped actual things at many places to keep things simple.

108
00:17:03.685 --> 00:17:13.427
We ignored many things particularly relevant for VR because, in VR, we are interested in a realistic experience where the complexity of the rendering is much more as

108.1
00:17:13.427 --> 00:17:18.262
compared to what we have seen in our discussion.

109
00:17:18.782 --> 00:17:30.197
Also, the scene is dynamic; it is changing based on user input, and those things we have not taken care of or discussed in the preceding discussion on the 3D graphics pipeline.

110
00:17:30.277 --> 00:17:32.460
So together all of these

111
00:17:34.178 --> 00:17:37.982
increase the computational complexity even more.

112
00:17:38.542 --> 00:17:45.929
So, in VR we require rich realistic content, and a simple lighting model may not be a good option.

113
00:17:47.451 --> 00:18:02.785
A high degree of interaction is present in VR, which we have not taken into account in our 3D graphics pipeline discussion, and there will be motion which is achieved through animation or video.

114
00:18:05.245 --> 00:18:18.527
So, all these together increase the computation for rendering in a VR environment manifold, and clearly that is the challenge: how to

114.1
00:18:18.527 --> 00:18:33.075
take care of such a computationally complex rendering process without letting the user perceive any lag or distortion.

115
00:18:35.958 --> 00:18:38.622
That is what we are interested in doing.

116
00:18:40.565 --> 00:18:45.152
So, from the preceding discussion, we got some idea of the computational complexity involved.

117
00:18:45.172 --> 00:18:52.563
Now, let us shift our focus to the implementation of such graphics pipelines for the benefit of the developer.

118
00:18:54.786 --> 00:18:59.153
Earlier, we outlined the theoretical background of computer graphics.

119
00:18:59.653 --> 00:19:02.758
Now, a programmer, a CG programmer, need not

120
00:19:03.717 --> 00:19:08.166
always implement the stages of the pipeline; that would be too much effort.

121
00:19:12.415 --> 00:19:19.650
Instead, the programmer can use APIs, application programming interfaces, provided by

122
00:19:20.592 --> 00:19:21.733
graphics libraries.

123
00:19:21.814 --> 00:19:25.098
So, there are already some implementations in the form of libraries.

124
00:19:25.278 --> 00:19:33.609
Programmers can import those libraries into the programming environment and use the APIs provided in those libraries to take care of the graphics-related concerns.

125
00:19:34.790 --> 00:19:47.106
Examples of such graphics libraries are OpenGL, which stands for Open Graphics Library, Vulkan, Direct3D, and so on.

126
00:19:51.440 --> 00:20:01.149
Now, these APIs are predefined sets of functions which, when invoked with appropriate arguments or parameters, perform specific tasks.

127
00:20:03.411 --> 00:20:12.772
So, these APIs eliminate the need to know every detail of the underlying system or platform, such as the processor details, memory details,

127.1
00:20:12.772 --> 00:20:18.105
OS details, and operating system details, and so on, to build a graphics application.

128
00:20:18.558 --> 00:20:27.632
If we are trying to build everything from scratch, we must have knowledge of these details, the hardware details on which our graphic system will work.

129
00:20:27.652 --> 00:20:32.740
However, that would be too much work every time somebody wants to develop a graphic application.

130
00:20:34.082 --> 00:20:46.742
Instead, they can take recourse to libraries and use the APIs provided in the libraries to deal with the lower-level tasks involved in computer graphics.

131
00:20:48.460 --> 00:20:55.588
For example, let us consider this OpenGL function glColor3frgb.

132
00:20:58.131 --> 00:21:05.198
This function is used to assign colors to a 3D point.

133
00:21:05.218 --> 00:21:17.472
Here, the programmer does not require to know the details such as how color is defined in the system, how many bits are there, what are the minimum values, maximum values, and all those things.

134
00:21:18.700 --> 00:21:29.306
how such information is stored and accessed, what kind of hardware support is provided to store it in the form of memory organization, and how to access that,

134.1
00:21:29.306 --> 00:21:40.827
what kind of instructions are provided to access that content of the memory, and how the OS manages the call to that memory.

135
00:21:40.867 --> 00:21:47.155
All these details are not needed to be known by the programmer

136
00:21:47.624 --> 00:21:52.910
to assign color to a point if the programmer chooses to use a library.

137
00:21:54.613 --> 00:22:05.219
Another piece of information required is which processor handles the task, whether CPU or GPU, and accordingly, instructions need to be executed.

137.1
00:22:05.219 --> 00:22:08.450
But then, the programmer does not need to know all these details.

138
00:22:10.232 --> 00:22:13.716
He can simply use this function, and

139
00:22:13.983 --> 00:22:21.373
the function takes care of all these details and returns the color to the programmer, the desired color to the programmer.

140
00:22:22.234 --> 00:22:27.361
That means to the application the programmer is using to call the function.

141
00:22:29.824 --> 00:22:40.058
So, that is how a programmer can think of implementing the pipeline, not by starting from scratch, but by knowing about different

142
00:22:42.215 --> 00:22:53.428
graphics libraries and knowing about the API supported by those libraries, and then choosing the one that suits the requirements and then using the APIs provided in that library.

143
00:22:53.449 --> 00:22:57.553
Now, let us try to put it in the perspective of the overall virtual reality system.

144
00:23:00.317 --> 00:23:07.986
Now, if we try to understand the system in a layered architecture form, then we have, at the top, the VR application layer.

145
00:23:10.649 --> 00:23:14.054
Below that is the software development kit or SDK layer.

146
00:23:16.298 --> 00:23:21.846
Below it will be the VR engine or the game engines.

147
00:23:24.851 --> 00:23:35.087
So, when I say this layer, that means the application layer calls the SDK layer, and the SDK layer calls the VR engine layer.

148
00:23:36.485 --> 00:23:45.517
Now, below the VR engine layer lie the native rendering libraries, such as OpenGL or Vulkan or Direct3D, such libraries.

149
00:23:46.117 --> 00:24:00.736
The VR engine, in turn, calls the library APIs to perform the rendering activities, and below it is the operating system layer and finally the hardware layer, including the GPU.

150
00:24:02.058 --> 00:24:05.462
So, essentially, if a VR system developer wants to

151
00:24:06.421 --> 00:24:17.373
Do something with the graphic system, then the developer does not need to bother much if he has access to such a layered system where

151.1
00:24:17.373 --> 00:24:28.215
he can take recourse to software development kits which access the VR engine. So the developer has to install the engine and import the

151.1.1
00:24:28.215 --> 00:24:35.297
development kit within the engine. Then whatever graphical operation is done, those are

152
00:24:36.003 --> 00:24:39.067
done through the APIs provided in the native rendering library.

153
00:24:39.567 --> 00:24:51.742
The job of the VR engine is to let the developer deal with graphics through those APIs without letting the developer directly access the native rendering libraries.

154
00:24:53.384 --> 00:24:59.752
And, of course, the native libraries sit on top of the operating system, which sits on top of the hardware layer.

155
00:25:01.454 --> 00:25:03.276
So, this is how we can visualize

156
00:25:05.450 --> 00:25:15.719
the implementation and use of graphics libraries in the context of VR system development, VR system deployment, or, in other words, immersive experience.

157
00:25:18.642 --> 00:25:27.470
This is the software side; that is how we can implement the graphic functions or graphic operations.

158
00:25:27.490 --> 00:25:33.195
On the hardware side, let us try to understand in a little more detail how GPUs are useful

159
00:25:34.525 --> 00:25:39.191
on the hardware side to make this process more efficient, to make this process faster.

160
00:25:43.818 --> 00:25:55.454
So, let us have a look at the role of the GPU in computer graphics in general and how that helps in virtual reality immersive experience in particular.

161
00:25:57.837 --> 00:26:01.342
So, the first thing that we should notice is the need for parallelism.

162
00:26:01.663 --> 00:26:04.026
Now, graphics operations are highly parallel.

163
00:26:05.660 --> 00:26:18.539
For example, modeling transformation: here what happens is that we apply transformations, such as rotation, to vertices; transformations in the form of matrices to the vertices.

164
00:26:19.881 --> 00:26:26.891
Now, a transformation is essentially a multiplication of a transformation matrix with the vertex vector.

165
00:26:29.455 --> 00:26:35.143
And this same vector-matrix multiplication is done for all vertices together.

166
00:26:35.444 --> 00:26:37.847
And ideally, we want to do it simultaneously.

167
00:26:37.867 --> 00:26:43.975
So, there is a parallelism involved here.

168
00:26:43.995 --> 00:26:52.766
So, we can have a significant gain in performance if we perform such multiplications on all vectors at the same time.

169
00:26:54.428 --> 00:26:59.815
If it is not done at the same time, then it will be much, much slower, and that

170
00:27:00.132 --> 00:27:06.720
significant gain is important in real-time rendering because millions of vertices need to be processed per second.

171
00:27:08.842 --> 00:27:13.107
The number may be even more when we are talking of a realistic immersive experience.

172
00:27:16.011 --> 00:27:21.377
Now, CPUs generally cannot take advantage of this inherent parallelism.

173
00:27:21.737 --> 00:27:26.663
Traditionally, the way CPUs used to be made, because they are not designed to do that.

174
00:27:27.183 --> 00:27:37.634
Of course, modern CPUs come with some such features, but they are traditionally not very good at taking care of large-scale parallel activities.

175
00:27:39.896 --> 00:27:48.425
So, modern graphics systems come with a separate graphics card, as we have already discussed earlier, containing their own processing unit and memory elements.

176
00:27:50.127 --> 00:27:54.692
And this processing unit is called a graphics processing unit or GPU, which

177
00:27:55.280 --> 00:27:59.866
is designed to deal with the inherent parallelism in graphics operations.

178
00:28:01.908 --> 00:28:19.110
Now, GPUs are typically multicore systems; that means they contain a large number of cores or unit processing elements, as shown here. You can see multiple cores are there in a GPU; they work on

179
00:28:19.698 --> 00:28:25.423
Single instructions: all the cores work simultaneously on an instruction and produce data simultaneously.

180
00:28:27.845 --> 00:28:41.718
So, each core is capable of performing simple integer and floating-point arithmetic operations only, and multiple cores group together to form what are called streaming multiprocessor units.

181
00:28:46.922 --> 00:28:47.663
So, we can have

182
00:28:48.318 --> 00:28:56.431
Broadly, we can view a GPU as organized in this way, as shown in this figure.

183
00:28:57.272 --> 00:29:07.548
We have the host, then input assemblers and a thread execution manager; then this manager allocates to different streaming multiprocessors.

184
00:29:09.752 --> 00:29:11.895
There can be a large number of such multiprocessors.

185
00:29:14.744 --> 00:29:18.891
which has a parallel data cache where the multiprocessor can produce its data.

186
00:29:20.153 --> 00:29:36.779
There is an element called texture storage, which essentially stores some information about predefined colors, surface colors, or predetermined surface colors.

187
00:29:36.799 --> 00:29:41.967
Then, these elements are accessed through the load and store information.

188
00:29:44.056 --> 00:29:53.218
The elements can access whatever data is produced; it can be sent to the global memory through the load information or store information, or

188.1
00:29:53.218 --> 00:30:01.904
the global memory content may be sent to this or accessed by these units through the load information.

189
00:30:01.924 --> 00:30:07.633
And each streaming multiprocessor has multiple cores, as shown here.

190
00:30:09.892 --> 00:30:14.158
So, a streaming multiprocessor contains several streaming processors or cores.

191
00:30:17.082 --> 00:30:17.843
So, how does it work?

192
00:30:18.564 --> 00:30:22.489
Now, most graphic systems assume a scene is made of triangles.

193
00:30:22.529 --> 00:30:25.473
The scene that we are trying to render is made of triangles.

194
00:30:25.853 --> 00:30:29.879
That means surfaces are converted to meshes, triangular meshes.

195
00:30:30.099 --> 00:30:39.612
Remember our discussion on object representation, where we said that eventually everything gets converted to meshes, irrespective of how a developer defines

196
00:30:40.655 --> 00:30:42.457
an object.

197
00:30:42.477 --> 00:30:49.723
So, the graphics processing unit assumes that the mesh is available as input.

198
00:30:51.365 --> 00:30:57.491
Now, through the APIs supported in the graphics library, triangles are sent to the GPU one vertex at a time.

199
00:30:57.511 --> 00:31:09.582
So, graphics libraries come with APIs using which such triangles in the mesh can be sent to the GPU one vertex at a time, and the GPU assembles vertices into triangles.

200
00:31:14.692 --> 00:31:18.096
Then the GPU performs modeling transformations on the vertices.

201
00:31:21.099 --> 00:31:31.851
So, multiple vertices are simultaneously transformed by multiplying the same matrix, the same transformation matrix, to all the set of vertices.

202
00:31:31.871 --> 00:31:41.222
The output is a stream of triangles, which are the transformed triangles from local coordinate to world coordinate.

203
00:31:42.805 --> 00:31:45.889
Next, the GPU computes the vertex color.

204
00:31:47.792 --> 00:31:55.743
Now, the color can be computed by vector dot products, as we have demonstrated during our discussion on the simple lighting model.

205
00:31:57.685 --> 00:32:03.754
So, the GPU performs such dot products for multiple vertices together simultaneously.

206
00:32:06.698 --> 00:32:09.842
After that, each colored vertex

207
00:32:10.430 --> 00:32:22.885
is projected onto the view plane; that is the projection transformation, which involves the viewing transformation and projection transformation together.

208
00:32:23.726 --> 00:32:29.693
So, the output is the stream of triangles in the screen or device coordinates ready to be converted to pixels.

209
00:32:30.414 --> 00:32:37.703
So, this projection includes all three transformations: view transformation, projection transformation, and viewport transformation.

210
00:32:38.257 --> 00:32:51.915
And the composite matrix is multiplied with vertices to get this final stream of triangles in the screen or device coordinates, which is a continuous coordinate space.

211
00:32:51.936 --> 00:32:55.741
Now, each device space triangle overlaps some pixels on the screen.

212
00:32:58.184 --> 00:33:05.614
In this rasterization stage, these pixels are determined, as we have discussed in stage 5.

213
00:33:07.265 --> 00:33:09.168
Now, there are many rasterization algorithms.

214
00:33:11.311 --> 00:33:18.001
All exploit one crucial observation: that is, each pixel can be treated independently from all other pixels.

215
00:33:19.903 --> 00:33:21.506
Again, that is inherent parallelism.

216
00:33:21.526 --> 00:33:24.710
So, this leads to handling all pixels in parallel.

217
00:33:27.494 --> 00:33:32.682
So, given the device space triangles, we can determine the color of all the pixels simultaneously.

218
00:33:34.636 --> 00:33:42.425
Now, during the pixel processing stage, two more activities take place: surface texturing and hidden surface removal.

219
00:33:44.587 --> 00:33:57.522
The simplest texturing method is to texture images that are already captured and stored, and apply the texture images to the geometry

220
00:33:58.582 --> 00:33:59.743
that we are trying to render.

221
00:34:00.124 --> 00:34:05.971
That means we simply replace the computed color with the color of the texture at the corresponding texture point.

222
00:34:08.935 --> 00:34:12.359
So, the pixel color is replaced by the texture color; that is the simplest method.

223
00:34:16.464 --> 00:34:20.569
In order to do that, GPUs store textures in high-speed memory.

224
00:34:21.710 --> 00:34:24.454
So, each pixel calculation must access it.

225
00:34:24.922 --> 00:34:36.938
Now, access is very regular; that means nearby pixels tend to access nearby texture image locations, and specialized memory caches are there to reduce the access time.

226
00:34:39.541 --> 00:34:44.728
Note that here we have this texture storage as part of the graphics card.

227
00:34:47.650 --> 00:34:54.018
Also, for hidden surface removal, GPUs implement a special algorithm called the

228
00:34:54.353 --> 00:35:02.802
depth buffer algorithm, which requires another storage that is called the depth buffer.

229
00:35:04.705 --> 00:35:22.144
So, GPUs contain such a depth buffer as a dedicated memory, which stores the depth information for each point in a 3D scene before they are projected onto a 2D plane.

230
00:35:22.164 --> 00:35:24.006
Depth is with respect to the pixel.

231
00:35:24.931 --> 00:35:31.659
Now, this memory, the depth buffer memory, stores distances of the viewer from each pixel.

232
00:35:35.144 --> 00:35:45.917
So, that is how GPUs work, and as you can see, GPUs are designed to take care of inherent parallelism; all the components that are there in the GPU are designed in that way, so

232.1
00:35:45.917 --> 00:35:54.027
that parallelly, the operations can be applied on all the vertices or a large number of vertices simultaneously to speed up things.

233
00:35:55.103 --> 00:36:05.643
So, in summary, what we have learned is that we have learned the overall 3D graphics pipeline, which is for static images, its stages,

233.1
00:36:05.643 --> 00:36:22.292
also learned how to implement the pipeline in software via libraries, and how GPUs can support the pipeline implementation by exploiting

234
00:36:24.263 --> 00:36:26.986
the inherent parallelism in the pipeline stages.

235
00:36:28.988 --> 00:36:39.533
In the next part of the lecture, we are going to talk about how all these ideas are put into practice, how all this knowledge helped us determine the complexity of a problem involved, and

235.1
00:36:39.533 --> 00:36:44.644
how we can try to address the complexities by taking recourse to different technologies.

236
00:36:46.266 --> 00:36:49.389
So, I hope you could follow the topic.

237
00:36:49.970 --> 00:36:52.853
Some parts, of course, we did not discuss in detail.

238
00:36:55.229 --> 00:37:03.002
So, I again urge you to go to the reference material for more details for a better understanding.

239
00:37:03.022 --> 00:37:13.238
However, our overall purpose was to give you some idea of the kind of computations involved and how we can utilize that knowledge in building the system.

240
00:37:14.163 --> 00:37:22.196
We have seen for libraries how we can place them so that the life of a developer becomes easier and the pipeline stages are implemented,

240.1
00:37:22.196 --> 00:37:26.516
without the developer needing to know every nitty-gritty of a graphics system.

241
00:37:27.598 --> 00:37:33.284
Also, we have briefly touched upon how GPUs help in the efficient implementation of the graphics pipeline.

242
00:37:35.686 --> 00:37:38.329
So, looking forward to meeting you again in the next part of the lecture.

